The '''History of Guajicutea (1538–1547)''' covers the first decade of the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]], from the end of the [[Great Pestilence]] through the consolidation of Burawi colonial control. The period was marked by violence against the former religious establishment and peasantry, the use of Guaji military forces as colonial enforcers by both the [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi]] and the [[Volisanian]]s, the First Capuyaquiran War (1540–1543) in which rebellious tributary peoples were incorporated as overseas territories, and the introduction of {{w|chattel slavery}} to the island's economy. The port of [[Waribá]] became a major transit point between the Volisanian [[Balboa]] colony and the Burawi [[Lacashe]] colony. The period ended in 1547 when Burawa shifted its military focus toward the [[Aruanãyára]] wars on the Abyala mainland.

== Violence and consolidation (1538–1543) ==

The establishment of the Kingdom of Guajicutea in 1536 was followed by a prolonged period of violence. Burawi forces, operating alongside the new king's loyalists, targeted peasants and former religious authorities — the bohíques and priestesses who had formed the spiritual backbone of the old order. The violence was both political and religious: the former religious establishment had been the primary opposition to the new regime during the [[Warí Buka]], and the Burawi mission had been actively suppressing traditional Guaji religious practice since the [[Dabra|first Dabra]] of 1532. Killings continued through the early 1540s as the new kingdom consolidated control.

The violence ended in 1543 through the intervention of Bishop [[Tekle Mariam]], the bishop of [[New Burawa]] — the colonial territory into which the Lacashe colony had been incorporated. Tekle Mariam pleaded with the Governor of Lacashe Province, [[Tewolde Berhe]], to halt the killings. The bishop's intervention reflected a tension within the colonial administration: the missionaries sought conversion, not extermination, and the ongoing violence undermined the church's ability to win converts among the surviving Guaji population. Governor Tewolde Berhe ordered the cessation of hostilities, and the violence subsided.

The period also saw increased transit between the Lacashe colony and Guajicutea. Guaji people — particularly those who had been educated or employed in Burawi service — traveled between the colony and the island more frequently, creating a small but growing class of cultural intermediaries.

== Waribá as a colonial transit hub ==

The port of [[Waribá]] became a major stoppage point for ships transiting between the Volisanian [[Balboa]] colony and the Burawi [[Lacashe]] colony. The route ran from [[Costabella]] in Balboa to Waribá (approximately 1,100 km), then from Waribá to [[Guledga]] in Lacashe (approximately 614 km). Waribá's protected inlet and existing trade infrastructure made it a natural resupply point at the midpoint of the journey.

The transit traffic brought economic activity to the port and increased contact between Guajicutea and both colonial powers. Ships stopping at Waribá carried goods, passengers, and correspondence between the two colonies, and the port's role as a waystation integrated Guajicutea into the colonial logistics network.

== The Burawi establishment ==

The Burawi colonial presence in Guajicutea was formalized during this period into two distinct institutions, both based in [[Waribá]]. The first Dabra — a hospice established under the 1532 agreement — had been the initial Burawi foothold on the island. After the establishment of the Kingdom of Guajicutea in 1536, the Burawi presence was expanded and reorganized into permanent institutions. In 1540, the original Dabra was moved to a larger mission complex on a hill near the port. The Emissary's fort, a fortified political headquarters at the port itself, was completed in 1544.

The religious establishment was the mission — a Dabra operated by the [[Baraka Order]], headed by a Guaji priest bearing the title of ''Liqa'' (head), a position created by the Burawi Catholic Church on the mainland. The Liqa was a Guaji convert who had been trained in Catholic doctrine and ordained to lead the mission. The mission was located on a hill near the port, physically separate from the secular settlement. The religious establishment relied primarily on Guaji labor — local converts and workers who maintained the mission compound, assisted in the hospice, and supported the missionary effort.

The political establishment was the Emissary of Burawa — a position created by the government of [[New Burawa]] to represent Burawi political interests in Guajicutea. The Emissary was based in a fort near the port area, serving as both residence and administrative center. Unlike the mission, the political establishment drew its workers from Burawa and New Burawa — bureaucrats, clerks, and support staff brought from the colonies rather than hired locally. The Emissary's role was to maintain the vassal relationship, oversee Burawi interests, and ensure that the kingdom's policies aligned with colonial strategy.

A Burawi military presence was maintained in both Waribá and the inland capital of [[Tabakurí]]. Three captains commanded three battalions — two stationed in Waribá and one in Tabakurí. The garrison's primary function was as a show of force: a visible reminder of Burawi military backing that kept the Guaji leadership compliant. The soldiers saw little action. Journals kept by Burawi officers stationed in Guajicutea describe the posting as a vacation compared to service in New Burawa, where Burawi forces were engaged on the front lines against the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]].

The Burawi population in Waribá was small but diverse — soldiers, missionaries, bureaucrats, and the families of non-military personnel. The mission and the fort formed two poles of colonial life: the religious community on the hill, the political-military establishment at the port, with the Guaji town between them. A census taken in 1547 recorded approximately 875 Burawi on the island: roughly 800 military (three battalions and officers), 20 mission staff, 15 political staff, and 40 family members of non-military personnel.

== Volisanian expansion and the 1537 agreement ==

Volisania expanded its colonial presence in the Capuyaquiran region during this period. In 1535, Volisania took the Tapuri island of [[Kartak]], establishing a foothold in the western Capuyaquiran. In 1538, Volisania subjugated the [[Boraku]] people and took their islands — including the former Guaji tributary islands of [[Barakú]], [[Weshina]], [[Tulami]], [[Kavura]], and [[Neroba]] — as a colonial possession. The Boraku had been Guaji tributaries since the 1330s–1360s; their loss to Volisania removed them from the Guaji sphere.

In 1537, Volisanian colonial authorities met with Boqor Petros I and signed an agreement of understanding. Records from the Balboa colony noted that Volisania sought to determine whether Guajicutea could be used to support their colonization efforts in the island chain and to pull influence away from Burawa. The agreement reflected Volisania's strategy of cultivating Guajicutea as a potential partner — or at minimum, a neutral party — in the competition with Burawa for dominance in the Capuyaquiran.

Volisania also began hiring Guaji sailors and soldiers as enforcers in their western island possessions. The Guaji — experienced navigators and fighters with deep knowledge of the Capuyaquiran islands, languages, and trade routes — were valuable intermediaries for colonial operations among peoples they had traded with for centuries.

== Guaji soldiers as colonial enforcers ==

During this period, Guajicutea's military force was effectively hired out as soldiers by both Burawa and Volisania. Guaji soldiers served as translators, enforcers of colonial authority, and overseers of subjugated peoples. Their knowledge of Capuyaquiran languages and customs made them more effective than European or African colonial troops in managing island populations.

The dual employment — serving both Burawi and Volisanian interests — placed Guajicutea in a complex position. The kingdom was a Burawi vassal, but its soldiers also served Volisanian colonial ventures. Boqor Petros I's agreement with Volisania in 1537 suggests that he was willing to play both sides, leveraging Guajicutea's strategic position and military utility to maintain autonomy within the colonial framework.

== First Capuyaquiran War (1540–1543) ==

{{main|First Capuyaquiran War}}

The weakening of Guajicutea during the Great Pestilence and the establishment of the new kingdom emboldened the tributary peoples to attempt independence. The Murani (islands of [[Maruba]], [[Dukira]], and [[Waturi]]), Dukuri ([[Karuba]]), and Barani ([[Bariba]]) — tributaries since the 1310s–1360s — refused continued subordination and launched what became known as the [[First Capuyaquiran War]].

The war was fought between a Burawi-Guaji alliance and the rebelling tributary polities. Burawi forces provided naval and military support; Guaji forces provided local knowledge, navigation, and manpower. The war lasted from 1540 to 1543.

By 1543, the Murani, Dukuri, and Barani had been defeated. Their islands were incorporated into Guajicutea as overseas territories — under Guajicutea's technical control but subject to Burawi authority. The conquered islands were collectively designated the [[Badweyn Islands]], named for [[Guled Badweyn]], the Burawi explorer who had first contacted Guajicutea in 1520. The incorporation expanded Guajicutea's territorial reach but bound it more tightly to Burawi colonial power: the overseas territories existed at Burawi sufferance, not by independent Guaji strength.

The [[Yakuri]] tributary on the island of [[Yakura]] (tributary since ~1290) is not recorded as having participated in the revolt. The Boraku tributaries ([[Barakú]], [[Weshina]], [[Tulami]], [[Kavura]], and [[Neroba]]) had already been lost to Volisania in 1538 and were not part of the conflict.

== Chattel slavery and the plantation economy ==

The Burawi slave trade, which began in 1533, brought enslaved Kaftians — people from the region of Sudanese Kaftia, north of [[Dakare]] in the continent of [[Kaftia]] — to the New World colonies. The trade was part of a broader pattern of Old World colonial powers replacing diminished native populations with enslaved labor. In the Capuyaquiran, the pestilence had reduced native populations across the islands, creating labor shortages that the slave trade was designed to fill.

Chattel slavery was introduced to the Guajicutea elite in October 1543, when the first enslaved Kaftian was brought to the port of Waribá and sold to the bikabári of [[Ganan]]. The introduction of slavery transformed the southern part of the island, which became a center of plantation farming. The southern bikabás — whose territories included the most suitable land for large-scale agriculture — developed {{w|plantation|plantations}} for cash crop production, using enslaved labor to work land that had been converted from communal farming to feudal holdings.

The shift from subsistence agriculture to plantation production was driven by the colonial trade economy. Cash crops could be exported to Burawi and Volisanian markets, generating wealth for the bikabáris who controlled the land. The southern bikabás who adopted plantation farming became the wealthiest of the feudal lords, their income from export crops exceeding that of the northern and central territories.

== Bikabá competition and the Bukan-Wakunan conflict ==

The feudal bikabás competed for wealth and influence. The wealthiest were the southern lords, who controlled the plantation economy, and the [[Bukan]] bikabá, which controlled access to the gold mines in the northern highlands. Gold had been the island's primary export for centuries, and the Bukan territory's position over the mining districts gave its bikabári outsized economic power.

The competition between bikabás produced armed conflict. A war broke out between the Bukan and the [[Wakunan]] bikabás over control of the gold mines. The conflict was mediated by Burawi forces, who intervened to prevent the disruption of gold exports — a key interest of the colonial administration. The Burawi mediation reinforced the kingdom's dependence on colonial authority: the bikabás could not resolve their disputes without foreign intervention, and the colonial power's interest in gold revenue took precedence over local political dynamics.

== The Sarta contact (May 1542) ==

In May 1542, a {{w|galleon}} from [[Sarta]] arrived at Guajicutea en route to the Balboa colony. Sarta was a smaller political power in the New World that had formed an alliance with Volisania and harbored ambitions of acquiring colonies. Sarta's leadership was scheming with Volisania to potentially take influence and territory from the Greater Capuyaquiran Sea, which was under Burawi and Guajicutea control.

The Sarta galleon's stop at Guajicutea was part of an effort to gain political favor with the Guaji leadership. Sarta sought to position itself as an alternative partner — a third power that could offer Guajicutea options beyond the Burawi-Volisanian rivalry. The extent of Sarta's success is unclear from surviving records, but the contact indicates that the colonial competition in the Capuyaquiran had attracted additional players beyond the two principal powers.

== Christianization ==

The 1538–1547 period was also the Christianization period. Catholicism was actively imposed on the Guaji population, but the form it took differed from standard Burawi Catholicism. The Guaji mapped their three powers — sea (creator), mountain (fertility and judgment), and sky (spirits and messengers) — onto the {{w|Trinity}}: God the Father, God the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The structural parallel — three powers, three persons — made the theological transition less alien than it might otherwise have been.

Cremation continued despite church opposition. The lunar calendar was officially replaced by the Burawi solar calendar, but with the introduction of {{w|paper}}, farmers and fishers continued tracking lunar cycles in written form — the calendar that had been carved in stone for a thousand years moved to a new medium, no longer sacred but practical. The healing and herbal knowledge of the bohíques was adopted by the Baraka Order missions for medicinal purposes, and some of this knowledge was translated back to Burawa.

Former priestesses (bohíques) could convert to nuns. Nunneries were established on Guajicutea, allowing the female religious tradition to survive in diminished form. Nuns had no political rights — unlike the Warí, who had held ultimate authority in Guaji society — but they could manage and establish nunneries. The women who had once confirmed kings now lived behind convent walls.

Baptism was adopted primarily among the elite and those connected to the Burawi administration. The general population remained largely unconverted during this period, though the institutional framework of the church — parishes, missions, and nunneries — was being established.

== End of the period (1547) ==

By 1547, the Kingdom of Guajicutea had been consolidated. The violence of the first decade had ended through Bishop Tekle Mariam's intervention. The First Capuyaquiran War had expanded the kingdom's territory. Chattel slavery had been introduced and the plantation economy was developing. The bikabá competition had been mediated. The transit economy through Waribá was established.

In 1547, Burawa began shifting its military focus toward the [[Aruanãyára]] wars on the Abyala mainland — the conflict that Yabí Tabá had predicted in his 1535 papers. The shift in Burawi attention away from the Capuyaquiran created space for Guajicutea to exercise greater autonomy within the colonial framework, but also reduced the military protection that Burawi forces had provided. The kingdom that emerged from this first decade was more territorial, more economically integrated with colonial trade, and more dependent on enslaved labor than the pre-colonial society it had replaced.

